==English==

===Etymology===
{{suffix|en|peculiar|ly}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/pɪˈkjuljɚli/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/pɪˈkjuːljəli/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-peculiarly.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyph|en|pe|cu|liar|ly}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# Such as to be greater than usual; [[particularly]]; [[exceptionally]].
## {{lb|en|degree}} To greater degree than is usual.
##: {{ux|en|He has a '''peculiarly''' strong accent.}}
##: {{ux|en|This racehorse is '''peculiarly''' fast.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1916|authorlink=John Dewey|first=John|last=Dewey|chapter=[[s:Democracy and Education/Section 14|Section 14]]|title=Democracy and Education|passage=Democratic society is '''peculiarly''' dependent for its maintenance upon the use in forming a course of study of criteria which are broadly human.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|year_published=1877|by={{w|Leo Tolstoy}}|author=[[w:Constance Garnett|Garnett, Constance]], transl.|chapter=[[s:Anna Karenina/Part One/Chapter 18|Chapter 18]]|title=Anna Karenina|original=Анна Каренина|others=part one|passage=He begged pardon, and was getting into the carriage, but felt he must glance at her once more; not that she was very beautiful, not on account of the elegance and modest grace which were apparent in her whole figure, but because in the expression of her charming face, as she passed close by him, there was something '''peculiarly''' caressing and soft.}}
## {{lb|en|manner}} In a manner that is greater than usual.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1818|authorlink=Mary Shelley|first=Mary|last=Shelley|chapter=[[s:Frankenstein/Chapter 4|Chapter 4]]|title=Frankenstein, or the Modern Prometheus|passage=One of the phenomena which had '''peculiarly''' attracted my attention was the structure of the human frame, and, indeed, any animal endued with life.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|date=1913-07-03|authorlink=Theodore Roosevelt|first=Theodore|last=Roosevelt|title=[[s:Address to the Boys Progressive League|Address to the Boys Progressive League]]|passage=I feel that the Progressive Party should appeal '''peculiarly''' to the young men--and therefore to the boys--who ought to be the next generation of voters.}}
# Such as to be [[strange]] or [[odd]].
## {{lb|en|degree}} [[strangely|Strangely]], [[oddly]].
##: {{ux|en|His nose is '''peculiarly''' bent.}}
## {{lb|en|manner}} In a [[strange]] or [[perverse]] manner; [[strangely]].
##: {{ux|en|He dresses '''peculiarly'''.}}
## {{lb|en|evaluative}} Such as to be [[strange]] or [[odd]].
##: {{ux|en|'''Peculiarly''', his hat is on upside-down.}}
## {{lb|en|act-related}} Acting in [[strange]] or [[perverse]] way.
##: {{ux|en|'''Peculiarly''', he left through the window.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Peculiarly''', he sat bolt upright and shouted "Geronimo!" whenever John Wayne appeared.}}
# Strongly associated with; in a manner that is peculiar or restricted to some person or place.
## {{lb|en|degree|of a place or circumstance}} Mostly or solely associated with.
##: {{ux|en|Having red hair and freckles is a characteristic that is '''peculiarly''' Northern European.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1907|authorlink=Swami Vivekananda|first=Swami|last=Vivekananda|chapter=[[s:The Complete Works of Swami Vivekananda/Volume 6/Lectures and Discourses/The Methods and Purpose of Religion|The Methods and Purpose of Religion]]|title=[[s:The Complete Works of Swami Vivekananda|The Complete Works of Swami Vivekananda]]|volume=6|passage=This is one of the methods of procedure. The other is through man to God. The second is '''peculiarly''' Aryan, and the first is '''peculiarly''' Semitic.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|author=[[w:Thomas Seltzer (translator)|Seltzer, Thomas]], transl.|title=[[s:Best Russian Short Stories/Introduction|Best Russian Short Stories]]|chapter=Introduction|passage=However, he already gave strong indication of the '''peculiarly''' Russian genius for naturalness or realism, and was a true Russian in his simplicity of style.}}
##* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1946|author=George Johnston|authorlink=George Johnston (novelist)|title=Skyscrapers in the Mist|page=52|text=This does not take into account the tens of thousands of New Yorkers who are harmlessly and charmingly insane in a '''peculiarly''' Manhattanite manner.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|in a manner that is greater than usual}} {{l|en|in particular}}, {{l|en|specifically}}; see also [[Thesaurus:specifically]]
* {{sense|in a strange or perverse manner}} {{l|en|oddly}}, {{l|en|queerly}}; see also [[Thesaurus:strangely]]

